# Project context for AI-assisted edits

## What this is
A Python port of a Snyk issue-migration correlation tool. Given a Snyk
project, it finds the predecessor project the same repo used to live at
before a migration (hostname change, re-import), and returns the
predecessor's open issues indexed by an identity that survives migration
(`attributes.key` for SCA, `fingerprint` for SAST), each with its true
`last_introduced_at`. Purpose: let a downstream SLA/grace-period check use
the real introduction date instead of the date Snyk assigns to the new
project (which resets to "now" after a migration).

There is no Snyk API to write `last_introduced_at` back onto an issue - this
tool produces a separate joinable dataset instead of mutating anything in Snyk.

## Architecture - preserve this split when editing
- `models.py`, `matching.py`, `resolver.py`, `client.py`, `correlate.py`:
  **pure logic only**. No `aiohttp`, no I/O, no network calls. Depends only
  on `typing.Protocol` (`ProjectsClient` in resolver.py, `SnykIssuesClient`
  in client.py) and plain dataclasses. This is the fully unit-tested layer
  (`tests/`, 13 tests, run with `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests`,
  no token or network needed) - keep it that way. Don't add `aiohttp`
  imports or real HTTP calls here.
- `snykapi.py`: the **only** file that talks to the real Snyk API.
  Implements the Protocols above. `SnykClient` in this file is a verbatim
  copy of the customer's existing `ear0_pharos.snyk.api.SnykClient` - if
  editing in the customer's actual repo, delete this copy and import theirs
  instead rather than maintaining two versions.
- `cli.py`: wires the real adapter to the pure logic. Thin - if it starts
  accumulating logic, that logic belongs in `correlate.py` or `resolver.py`
  instead.

## Matching rule (don't weaken this without reason)
Repo identity = `org/repo`, normalized from `attributes.url` (strip scheme,
host, `.git` suffix) or `attributes.display_name` (strip anything from `(`
onward - CLI manifest suffixes look like `org/repo(main):requirements.txt`).
Predecessor = same repo identity, **same product** (SCA/SAST don't
cross-match), oldest `created_at` that's still older than the given project.

## Known-unverified assumptions in snykapi.py (fix here first if something breaks)
1. `display_name` as a Targets API filter param - unconfirmed against a real response.
2. `attributes.type == "sast"` as the signal for a Snyk Code project vs.
   anything else being SCA - unconfirmed.
3. Projects API using `attributes.created` (not `created_at`, which is
   confirmed correct on the Issues API from the customer's own
   `parse_issues_data`) - unconfirmed, worth checking a real project response.
4. `LiveResolver` only searches within one org - group/cross-org migrations
   aren't handled.

If something is wrong at runtime, it is almost certainly one of these four
things in `snykapi.py`, not the matching/correlation logic in the other files.

## Don't do this
- Don't add a write/PATCH call for `last_introduced_at` anywhere - Snyk's
  Issues API doesn't support it; this was already checked.
- Don't fetch or modify the *new* project's issues from inside this tool -
  that stays entirely on the customer's existing side, by design.
- Don't collapse the product check out of the resolver's matching, even if
  it looks redundant with the repo-key check - SCA and SAST projects for
  the same repo can have different lifecycles.

See `README.md` for the full narrative version of all of the above.
